  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several applications of a porous or functionalized fibrous material decorated with small lattice-like particles. Across all panels, the same wavy fiber scaffold is shown acting as a platform for different tasks, suggesting a versatile multifunctional material.\n\nIts roles include filtration and protective capture of toxic molecules, where harmful species are trapped on the surface while safer products or clean flow pass through. The material also appears to have antimicrobial activity, interacting with viruses and bacteria and generating reactive oxygen species (ROS). Another panel highlights flame resistance, implying the coating helps block heat or combustion.\n\nFurther uses are in catalysis, where reactants contact the supported particles and are converted into products, and in energy storage, where lithium ions are shown associating with the material. In sensing, toxic molecules bind to the surface and produce an electronic signal, indicated by electrons/lightning symbols. Finally, the scaffold is shown carrying and releasing drug molecules, linked to wound-healing applications.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that this particle-coated fiber network can adsorb, protect, disinfect, resist fire, catalyze reactions, store ions, detect chemicals, and deliver therapeutics.",
